  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest radar and satellite imagery depict \nanother large cluster of showers and thunderstorms towards the west \nacross Indiana, with SPC mesoanalysis revealing a tight MLCAPE \ngradient oriented generally NNW to SSE. Convection trends have \nalready begun following this gradient, with the stronger and more \norganized storms beginning to dip further south across Indiana and \nsouthwest Ohio. Modest residual westerly 850 mb flow of 30 to 35 \nknots across Northwest Ohio may allow for some showers and storms to \nredevelop this afternoon, though the threat for any stronger storms \nacross our area appears to be decreasing. There is a somewhat low \npotential for some heavier rain and isolated flash flooding later \nthis evening as a trough swings south through the Eastern Great \nLakes, as the arriving 850 mb jet may result in a stalled boundary \nsomewhere across far Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania.\n\nOtherwise, the heavy rain threat for Thursday night into Friday \nappears to be decreasing as QPF guidance continues to trend further \nsouth into the Ohio Valley. Could still see some scattered showers \nand storms Thursday night into Friday, particularly along or near \nthe US-30 corridor, but the overall heavy rain threat appears to be \ndecreasing. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe next period for unsettled weather will be \nSaturday night through Sunday as a low pressure system moves east \nthrough the Great Lakes, extending a cold front across the area \nSunday night. Will continue to monitor the threat for any stronger \nstorms, especially Sunday afternoon with the latest guidance \nsuggesting a potent 700 mb shortwave of 35 to 40 knots entering the \narea.",
